WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:01.780
- [Instructor] So I have data here

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.490
on two different websites,

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.820
website A and website B.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.170
And my question to you is,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.620
which one is more productive?

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.180
And some of you might be asking yourself,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.140
what does it mean

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.720
to be productive?

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.110
And at a very high level,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.820
you could view productivity
as how effective something

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.400
is at producing.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.820
So for a given amount of input

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.890
into the process,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.420
how much output are you getting?

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.860
If you want to get a
little bit more exact,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.260
you could view it as the
rate of output per input.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.370
But that still makes us ask the question,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.290
what are the inputs and
what are the outputs?

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.690
So pause this video and think about

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.230
how you would measure productivity for or

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.140
how you would compare the productivity of

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.430
these two websites.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.840
And there's multiple ways

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.673
to do it.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:55.830
All right, now let's
think through it together.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:56.970
What I'm first going to do

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.620
is look at each of these lines and think

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.130
about whether they are
an input or an output.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.930
So the number of writers, well,

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.040
you need the writers

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.020
to produce the website.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.880
So I would consider that an input.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.740
Although if you were the head of HR,

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.550
the person who is hiring writers,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.860
the head of human resources,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.280
and you were hiring writers,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.600
then that could actually be an output.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.680
But if we're just thinking

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.720
about creating a website,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.650
we need writers to create
it so that's an input.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.260
Number of posts posted,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.570
average number of words of post,

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.900
average likes of post,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.790
average comments of post,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.380
number of new subscribers,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.230
those all feel like outputs.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.960
If the writers do a good job,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.620
they're going to produce a lot of posts,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.050
they're going to, I guess,
have a lot of words,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:40.883
I don't know if it's good

00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.070
to always be wordy.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:43.810
People will like their posts,

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.110
they'll comment on those posts.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.890
We'll have new subscribers.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.663
So this is all output.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.340
What about revenue?

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.490
That's how much money a site brings

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.530
in before having to pay its expenses.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.620
So that also should be an output

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.470
because if the writers do a good job,

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.040
if the site is well run,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.860
they will generate a lot of revenue.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.140
What about expenses?

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.780
Well, you need to use expenses.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.980
You need to spend expenses on
things like writer salaries in

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.140
order to create your website,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.040
in order to generate that revenue.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.520
So I would consider that an input.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.430
And what about profit?

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.511
Profit is all the money you bring in

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:28.780
minus your expenses and
how much you get leftover.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:29.613
Well,

00:02:29.613 --> 00:02:31.630
some people would argue the
whole point of a business

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.960
is to generate that profit.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.020
That is an outcome you want
to maximize, an output.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.083
And so I would consider that an output.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.890
And so the question of
productivity really just boils down

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.330
to which output or what combinations

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.330
of output do we want
to figure out the rate

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.500
that we're generating
relative to some input or

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.560
some combination of inputs?

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.850
So a really simple way
of measuring productivity

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:57.900
in this situation,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.810
let's define it, productivity.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:01.643
And once again,

00:03:01.643 --> 00:03:03.440
there's multiple ways of defining it.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.170
Let's say it is equal

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:10.170
to the profit per number of writers.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:15.770
So it could be profit over
the number of writers.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.250
If we define it that way,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.403
which website is more productive?

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.860
Well, website A,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.560
in that definition,

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.490
A would have a productivity

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.760
of $10,000 for five writers.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.760
So it would be $2,000 profit per writer.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.800
And what would be B's per
activity by this definition?

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.020
Well, $28,000 divided by 11,

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.640
28,000 divided by 11 writers,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.550
there's $28,000 in profit.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.200
That gives us a 2,000,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.620
and we'll round, 2,545.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.620
So approximately $2,545
of profit per writer.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.040
So based on this
measurement of productivity,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.000
it looks like website B

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.773
is more productive.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.870
Now what if we did maybe
even a simpler one,

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.250
or maybe one that you might think

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.170
is even more natural?

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.420
What if we thought about it as the number

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.210
of posts per writer?

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.540
So let's do it that way.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.860
So if we just defined productivity,

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.693
I'll just do that,

00:04:28.693 --> 00:04:31.700
as being equal to the number

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.700
of posts divided by the writers.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.850
Well, in this situation,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.654
website A would have a productivity

00:04:40.654 --> 00:04:44.690
of 110 divided by five,

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.690
which would be 22,

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:51.590
22 posts per writer,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.897
and website B 200 divided by 11 ,

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.300
200 divided by 11 gets us,

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.030
well, we could just
round that to maybe 18,

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:04.290
since we want to get rough estimates,

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:09.290
to 18 posts per writer.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.460
So by this measure of productivity,

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.710
website A actually looks more productive.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.580
So hopefully this gives
you just a general sense

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:18.890
of what productivity is,

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.380
and an appreciation that
there's multiple ways

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:22.290
of measuring it.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.320
And you could do much fancier things,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.340
you could say,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.580
you could make it a whole combination

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.180
of outputs divided by a
combination of inputs.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.820
So you could define
productivity as the number

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.570
of posts posted times the
average number of posts,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:37.820
you take that quantity,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.610
and then maybe add the
average likes per post

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.910
or multiply by the average likes per post,

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:46.160
and then add to that the comments per post

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.050
or multiply by the comments per post

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.040
and then divide by the number of writers

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.870
or the number of writers and expenses

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.630
or divide just by the expenses.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:58.170
But the general notion is you
wanna take some combination

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.003
of the outputs,

00:05:59.003 --> 00:06:00.720
maybe even just one
output and divide it by

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.630
some combination of the inputs
and you can actually decide

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.440
how you even wanna combine,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.220
how you wanna mathematically combine

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.763
those outputs and inputs.

